The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
handler = create_flow_rule(dev, ft_prio, &leftovers_specs[LEFTOVERS_MC].flow_attr, dst);
if (!IS_ERR(handler) && flow_attr->type == IB_FLOW_ATTR_ALL_DEFAULT) {
    handler_ucast = create_flow_rule(dev, ft_prio, &leftovers_specs[LEFTOVERS_UC].flow_attr, dst);
}
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because when the creation of the unicast flow rule (`handler_ucast`) fails, there is no cleanup for the previously allocated multicast flow rule (`handler`). This results in a resource leak since the allocated multicast rule remains in memory without being freed.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (IS_ERR(handler_ucast)) {
    mlx5_del_flow_rule(handler->rule);
    kfree(handler);
}
```
Reason:
The fix includes a cleanup step that deletes the multicast flow rule (`handler->rule`) and frees the associated memory (`kfree(handler)`) if the creation of the unicast flow rule fails. This ensures that resources are properly managed and prevents the resource leak.

